WEBVTT

00:03.920 --> 00:04.280
All right.

00:04.280 --> 00:06.680
So now let's say that we have a table for the customers.

00:06.680 --> 00:09.600
We have three columns ID name and status.

00:09.600 --> 00:13.320
And as well we have around 2 million rows 2 million customers.

00:13.320 --> 00:18.960
And as we learned as a default, the table can be built as a heap structure where the rows are stored

00:19.000 --> 00:20.760
row by row inside data pages.

00:20.760 --> 00:25.280
But now we go and create a column store index on top of this table.

00:25.280 --> 00:31.200
So now once you do that, SQL gonna go through a process in order to build the column store.

00:31.240 --> 00:36.600
So the first step is, is go and divide the data the rows into row groups.

00:36.640 --> 00:41.120
Now in SQL server each row group can contain around like 1 million row.

00:41.120 --> 00:45.160
So in this example our table can be split into two row groups.

00:45.160 --> 00:49.440
The first 1 million row in one group and the second one in another row group.

00:49.440 --> 00:53.400
Now you might ask me, we are talking about columns why we are splitting the rows.

00:53.440 --> 00:59.080
Well, this is just a pre step in order just to optimize the performance and to do parallel processing.

00:59.120 --> 01:03.320
And of course the data will not be stored like this because we have the second step.

01:03.360 --> 01:07.290
Now in the next step is SQL going to go and segment the columns.

01:07.290 --> 01:12.810
So now scale will go for each row group and start splitting the data by the columns.

01:12.850 --> 01:18.170
And that's why we call it a column store because we are separating the columns from each others.

01:18.170 --> 01:24.490
So that means we have one segment for the ID, another one for the name, and a third one for the status.

01:24.490 --> 01:26.490
And this can happen for each row group.

01:26.530 --> 01:28.290
And now scale move to the third step.

01:28.290 --> 01:31.410
In this process we have the data compression.

01:31.410 --> 01:37.930
And this is the most important step in this process because it is the reason why column store is very

01:37.930 --> 01:40.290
fast compared to the roster.

01:40.330 --> 01:44.730
So in this process there are like different techniques on how to do data compression.

01:44.730 --> 01:48.890
And the most famous one is that it's going to go and create like a dictionary.

01:48.890 --> 01:55.370
Let's take for example the column status, the status of the customer, whether it is active or inactive.

01:55.370 --> 02:01.890
So the word active and inactive can be repeated like 2 million times because we have 2 million customers.

02:01.930 --> 02:05.890
And since it is like string, it is like taking a lot of space and storage.

02:05.890 --> 02:08.970
But now instead of that, we're going to go and compress the data.

02:08.970 --> 02:16.180
So first it's going to go and create a dictionary by replacing the value active and inactive into smaller

02:16.180 --> 02:18.060
values like one and two.

02:18.180 --> 02:22.540
So we have like a mapping between the long value to small value.

02:22.540 --> 02:28.780
And after that it's going to store like a data stream where we have like only two values 121, two.

02:28.940 --> 02:31.620
So we can have like a big stream of 2 million rows.

02:31.660 --> 02:34.460
So SQL going to go and do this for each column.

02:34.460 --> 02:37.500
And with that the size of each column is going to be changed.

02:37.540 --> 02:41.220
Depends of course on how much different values you have in each column.

02:41.460 --> 02:47.740
So this step is very important in order to reduce the size of the data and as well to increase the performance.

02:47.860 --> 02:54.060
So now once everything is organized and compressed, SQL is going to go and start storing the results

02:54.060 --> 02:55.300
in databases.

02:55.460 --> 02:59.940
But SQL will not use the standard databases that we have learned previously.

02:59.940 --> 03:05.420
But instead it's going to use a special database called Lob Large Object Page.

03:05.460 --> 03:10.260
So now let's quickly compare the structure of the normal database that we have learned in the row store

03:10.300 --> 03:13.660
with the new one that column store the Lob data page.

03:13.740 --> 03:15.870
So as usual each page has a header.

03:15.910 --> 03:20.350
This is same as any data page, but the next section is going to be the segment header.

03:20.390 --> 03:25.990
It has like metadata informations about the column segment that is stored in this page.

03:25.990 --> 03:32.830
Like we have the segment ID, the row group ID, the column ID and it has as well very important information

03:33.070 --> 03:35.470
the ID to the dictionary page.

03:35.550 --> 03:39.590
So the dictionary page is as well a type of pages in SQL.

03:39.630 --> 03:41.190
It has as well a header.

03:41.190 --> 03:43.310
But inside it we have like a mapping.

03:43.390 --> 03:49.630
So it maps the original value that long one the inactive to the smaller version of this value.

03:49.750 --> 03:50.830
For example one.

03:50.830 --> 03:52.590
And that's all for the dictionary page.

03:52.590 --> 03:56.710
It has the mapping between the original values and the smaller values.

03:56.710 --> 03:59.110
And beneath the segment header we're going to have.

03:59.150 --> 04:01.630
Now the important place where our data can be stored.

04:01.630 --> 04:03.150
We have the data stream.

04:03.150 --> 04:10.390
So it is like a sequence of IDs from the dictionary that represents the values of the columns side by

04:10.390 --> 04:10.830
side.

04:10.830 --> 04:15.630
And of course we cannot fit the whole 1 million rows inside this data stream.

04:15.630 --> 04:18.550
We're going to have like multiple Lob databases.

04:18.550 --> 04:21.800
So this is how exactly the SQL stores your data.

04:22.080 --> 04:24.360
If you decided to go with the column store.

04:24.400 --> 04:26.160
So let's go back to the process.

04:26.280 --> 04:27.680
So back to the process.

04:27.680 --> 04:31.760
As you can see SQL is storing the data as a blob data storage.

04:31.760 --> 04:33.160
So this is the last step.

04:33.160 --> 04:37.240
And with that SQL did convert your table into a column store.

04:37.280 --> 04:43.320
So now we cannot just create a column store without defining whether it is clustered index or non-clustered

04:43.320 --> 04:43.960
index.

04:43.960 --> 04:47.040
So let's start with the first one the clustered column store index.

04:47.280 --> 04:52.840
So if you create such index, SQL of course will not be building a B-tree structure.

04:52.960 --> 04:57.080
SQL is going to use exactly this structure, the column store structure.

04:57.080 --> 05:01.440
So as we learned, the clustered index is a complete makeover of your table.

05:01.440 --> 05:02.280
When you applied.

05:02.320 --> 05:09.320
Then SQL can format everything column wise, and it is fully replacing the old row based table structure

05:09.320 --> 05:10.560
that we have at the start.

05:10.560 --> 05:15.480
So once you apply the clustered column store index, it will not leave anything behind.

05:15.520 --> 05:19.000
And your table is going to be completely structured as a column store.

05:19.040 --> 05:20.680
And one more thing, which is make sense.

05:20.680 --> 05:27.010
Of course all the columns from the original table can be converted to a column store, so it is not

05:27.010 --> 05:28.370
leaving anything behind it.

05:28.370 --> 05:34.410
But in the other hand, if you are using non-clustered column store index, as we learned, it is like

05:34.410 --> 05:37.090
a companion to your existing table.

05:37.090 --> 05:41.650
So it co-exists with the table and it will not replace anything.

05:41.650 --> 05:47.410
So the column store index can be an additional thing that is stored beside your table.

05:47.410 --> 05:52.970
So that means the original table will not be deleted at all like the clustered column store index.

05:53.010 --> 05:56.210
The first one is in the old row based storage.

05:56.210 --> 05:57.250
The regular table.

05:57.290 --> 05:58.570
The first one and your.

05:58.570 --> 06:03.530
That is going to be as well stored in a separate structure in the column store index.

06:03.530 --> 06:06.530
And of course in the non-clustered column store index.

06:06.530 --> 06:12.690
Since we are creating an extra index outside of your original table, you can go and define which column

06:12.690 --> 06:14.530
should be included in this process.

06:14.530 --> 06:18.490
It must not be all the columns you can go, for example, with only the status.

06:18.490 --> 06:24.290
So that means you build a column store index only for one column for the status of their customers.

06:24.290 --> 06:30.010
So this is what we mean with the clustered column store index and the non-clustered column store index.
